        {
          "id": 1254630516,
          "type": "DiffNote",
          "body": "Yes, you're right, and there's an associated bug filed for it.\n\nThe problem is there's two different situations for protocols following each other: tunnelled inside the previous protocol at a higher level, or at the same level as an additional PDU contained within a third lower level protocol.\n\nWe don't really distinguish the two, and it causes a number of issues. (The Protocol Hierarchy stats also have problems with this.) The issue with multiple PDUs carried within PPP was crashing.\n\nEven addresses and ports based conversations don't work extremely well with tunneling, like TCP inside a GTP tunnel on UDP, there's no good way to follow the outer UDP stream (or indicate it on the GUI), and there's not a lot of good ways to deal with IP with ICMP that sometimes has rejected packets, along with other packets associated with the tunnelled rejected packets.\n\n`find_conversation_pinfo` is sometimes used for \"find the last conversation set in this packet\" and sometimes for \"find the current conversation for this packet,\" but for multiple PDUs those are not the same thing. And sometimes it is used for \"find any X protocol conversation in this frame,\" which doesn't work with tunneling.",
          "author": {
            "id": 6764551,
            "username": "johnthacker",
            "public_email": "",
            "name": "John Thacker",
            "state": "active",
            "locked": false,
            "avatar_url": "https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/99c30adacedf45514aaf711ba8a44f56322ebb53e9b550b7e3c9cd83bd6a1408?s=80&d=identicon",
            "web_url": "https://gitlab.com/johnthacker"
          },
